---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Related from "../components/Related.astro";
import { SITE, IDENTITY } from "../lib/site.ts";

/**
 * TERMS AND A DISCLAIMER, ON ONE PAGE, BECAUSE THERE IS ALMOST NOTHING TO SAY.
 *
 * Added after a third-party assessment listed "Terms of Service, Privacy Policy and a
 * Disclaimer stating the site provides no financial or custodial services" among the things
 * that would raise trust. Two of the three were already here. The third is worth adding for a
 * reason that has nothing to do with box-ticking: on this site the disclaimer is TRUE in a
 * structural sense rather than a legal one. A site that cannot take a deposit is not promising
 * not to; it is describing what it is.
 *
 * The same assessment recommended a legal entity and a registered address. That is refused, on
 * the page, in writing — see below. There is no company, and inventing one would be the single
 * most falsifiable claim this site could make. A stated absence is stronger than an invented
 * presence, and it is checkable: there is no registration to find.
 *
 * Deliberately short. Terms that nobody reads are not a trust signal, and length here would be
 * padding around three real sentences.
 */
const title = "Terms and disclaimer";
const description =
  `${SITE.name} provides no financial, custodial or advisory service. It holds no funds, takes no ` +
  `payments and cannot execute anything. What the site is, in plain terms.`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb={{ href: "/about", label: "About", current: "Terms and disclaimer" }}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/about">About</a> › Terms and disclaimer</p>
    <h1>Terms and disclaimer</h1>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">
    {/* THE SUMMARY SAID MORE THAN THE SITE CAN. "It does not hold anything of yours, cannot receive
        anything from you" (and "holds nothing, receives nothing" in the description) sat on the page
        that points to /privacy, which says serving a page means the host handles your IP address and
        the URL you asked for, that calculator inputs reach request logs, and that Google Analytics
        sends page views to Google. The claim-vs-table audit of 16 September 2026 confirmed the
        contradiction. What is structurally true is narrower: no money, no credentials, no account. */}
    This page is short because there is little to agree to. {SITE.name} publishes numbers as
    ordinary web pages. It holds no funds or assets of yours, cannot take a payment or a
    credential from you, and has no account for you to create. What an ordinary page request does
    send, and what the site's analytics collect, is set out on{" "}
    <a class="link" href="/privacy">Privacy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">No financial service, in the structural sense</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    {SITE.name} is <strong>not</strong> an exchange, broker, dealer, custodian, wallet, payment
    processor or investment adviser, and provides no financial, custodial or advisory service of
    any kind. It executes no orders, holds no funds or assets for anyone, and offers no
    mechanism by which money or credentials could reach it — there is no account, no deposit
    path, no wallet connection, no payment form and nothing to sign.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    That is a description of how the site is built rather than a promise about how it behaves,
    which means you do not have to believe it.
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/about">About</a> lists what can be checked in your own browser,
    including that there is no account to create.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Nothing here is advice</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Everything published is market data and arithmetic on market data. None of it is investment,
    financial, legal or tax advice, a recommendation, a solicitation, or an opinion about what
    anyone should do. Figures described as <em>modelled</em> — liquidation levels in particular —
    are the output of stated assumptions applied to public inputs, printed alongside those
    assumptions, and are not predictions. The liquidation map prints its assumptions on the map page
    itself; <a class="link" href="/methodology">Methodology</a> shows how funding rates are
    annualised.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    This applies in full to every figure the site <em>derives</em> rather than receives, and those
    are the ones most easily mistaken for a view: annualised funding rates, the cross-venue funding
    spread and every figure on <a class="link" href="/tools">Tools</a>, modelled liquidation levels,
    the survival table, the sweep order, and the output of all four calculators. Each is arithmetic
    on an input under assumptions you can read and change — not a forecast, not a signal, and not a
    statement that any position is a good idea. A calculator that computes what a trade would cost
    is not an opinion that the trade is worth making.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Accuracy, and its limits</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    The data comes from third-party endpoints named on
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/data-sources">Data sources</a>, and is republished as received
    apart from the normalisation described in the methodology. Upstreams fail, lag and revise;
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/status">Status</a> publishes the real success rate of the ingest,
    failures included. Every figure states its own age. No warranty of accuracy, completeness or
    fitness for any purpose is offered — not for the data as received, and equally not for anything
    derived or modelled from it — and decisions taken on the basis of anything here are yours alone.
    Where an upstream disclaims the accuracy of its own numbers, that disclaimer travels: a figure
    computed from an input carries every limit the input carried, plus the limits of the assumptions
    applied to it.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    If a number here disagrees with the venue it came from, that is a bug and we want to know:
    {" "}<!--email_off--><a class="link" href={`mailto:${IDENTITY.contact}`}>{IDENTITY.contact}</a><!--email_on-->.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">There is no company, and that is stated rather than obscured</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    {SITE.name} is an independently operated project, not a registered company, and it has no
    legal entity, no registered address and no corporate identity. It was suggested that
    publishing one would raise trust. It would not: a company that does not exist is the most
    easily falsified claim this site could make, and every other statement here is written to be
    checkable. {IDENTITY.founder ? `The project is run by ${IDENTITY.founder.name}` : "The project is independently run"},
    and the accountable contact is the address on this domain.
  </p>

  <h2 class="section">Use of the site</h2>
  <p class="prose">
    Read it and link to it freely. Quote it with attribution, and automated access is welcome
    within reason — {" "}<a class="link" href="/robots.txt">robots.txt</a> lists the crawlers that
    are asked to stay away. Do not present the data as your own, and do not rely on it as
    a sole source for anything that matters. The site may change or stop publishing at any time;
    where a URL is withdrawn it is withdrawn honestly, with a 410 rather than a redirect.
  </p>
  {/* THIS SECTION USED TO GRANT REUSE OVER EVERYTHING ON THE PAGE, WITHOUT DISTINGUISHING
      WHAT WE COMPUTE FROM WHAT WE RECEIVE. Read literally it sub-licensed upstream market data
      to third parties — a right nobody here holds, from a project with no legal entity to hold
      it. The permission is now split along the only line that matters: the arithmetic is ours
      to share, the inputs are not. */}
  <p class="prose">
    That permission covers what this site <strong>computes</strong>: annualised rates, spreads,
    modelled levels, the survival table, calculator outputs, and the words around them. It does
    not, and cannot, cover the <strong>upstream data</strong> underneath. Funding rates, marks,
    open interest and on-chain contract state belong to the venues and networks that published
    them, and reach you under their terms rather than ours.
    {" "}<a class="link" href="/data-sources">Data sources</a> names each one and the endpoint every
    figure came from, and links the venue terms that could be found. If you intend to republish upstream figures — at scale, or
    commercially — read the venue's terms. Nothing on this page grants you anything in respect of
    them, and nothing on this page is a representation that they permit it.
  </p>
  <p class="prose">
    {/* "NO PERSONAL DATA IS COLLECTED" WAS NOT TRUE, AND THIS IS THE LEGAL PAGE. /privacy lists a
        Google Analytics cookie that is "a random identifier for your browser", says GA4 "sends data
        to Google, a third party", and says the host handles your IP address. An identifier and an IP
        address can be personal data. The sentence now says what the site does not do and sends the
        reader to the page that states what it does — it does not assert an absence it cannot prove. */}
    There are no accounts, so there is no profile here to return or delete. Some data is processed
    when you use the site: the host handles your IP address and the address of each page you
    request, and Google Analytics sets an identifier in your browser and sends page views to Google.
    What each of these is, and how to block the analytics, is set out on{" "}
    <a class="link" href="/privacy">Privacy</a>.
  </p>

  <Related items={[
    { href: "/about", label: "About", note: "What the site is, what it structurally is not, and how to check both." },
    { href: "/privacy", label: "Privacy", note: "What is collected, in specifics rather than assurances." },
    { href: "/methodology", label: "Methodology", note: "How funding rates are annualised, and what is deliberately absent." },
    { href: "/data-sources", label: "Data sources", note: "Every upstream endpoint, its cadence, and what is deliberately absent." },
  ]} />
</Base>
